 abstract = {The performance of parallel code significantly depends the parallel task granularity (PTG). If the PTG is too coar performance suffers due to load imbalance; if the PTG is t fine, performance suffers from the overhead that is induc by parallel task creation and scheduling. This paper presents a software platform that automa cally determines the PTG at run-time. Automatic PTG sel tion is enabled by concurrent calls, which are special sour language constructs that provide a late decision (at run-tim of whether concurrent calls are executed sequentially or co currently (as a parallel task). Furthermore, the execution mantics of concurrent calls permits the runtime system merge two (or more) concurrent calls thereby coarseni the PTG. We present an integration of concurrent calls in the Java programming language, the Java Memory Mod and show how the Java Virtual Machine can adapt the PT based on dynamic profiling. The performance evaluati shows that our runtime system performs competitively Java programs for which the PTG is tuned manually. Co pared to an unfortunate choice of the PTG, this approa performs up to 3x faster than standard Java code. Copyright © 2013. Copyright © 2013 ACM.},
